From: grigsby@agames.com (JUMP IN THE FIRE)
Date: Tue, 30 Aug 1994 10:31:15 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-8-302] PSYCH: Seeing power flicker

> Let me add my own flaky claim. Sometimes, after waking up at night and
> turning on the lights, I could swear that I am seeing the fluctuation
> of the light (presumably at 60 Hz) in my peripheral vision. Funny
> thing is, I kind of doubt that a light filament can actually respond
> at 60Hz.

Light filaments respond at 120Hz.  The sine wave described by the
voltage lights the lamp whether it is swinging positive or negative,
thus doubling the frequency.

 /\              /\  /\
/  \      --->  /  \/  \
    \  /
     \/

(ok, so I can't draw a sine wave in ASCII)

> I am seeing something at the outside edges of my vision,
> though. Anybody else get this?

I can see 60Hz monitors flicker if I don't look straight at them.
European (50Hz) monitors drive me nuts, I can see the flicker
all the time.  Of course, since most good X terminals/workstations
refresh around 70Hz these days I don't notice this as much anymore.

Movies don't work well for me at all, especially if there's fast
motion involved.  24Hz is just not enough.

